WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:11.280
This new paper fixes many common problems when it&nbsp;
comes to two-way coupling in fluid simulations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.720
And of course, the first question is, what&nbsp;
is two-way coupling? It means that here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.880
the boxes are allowed to move the smoke, and&nbsp;
the added two-way coupling part means that now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.240
the smoke is also allowed to blow away the boxes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.640
What’s more, the vortices here on the right&nbsp;
are even able to suspend the red box in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.080
the air for a few seconds. An excellent&nbsp;
demonstration of a beautiful phenomenon.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.880
However, simulating this effect&nbsp;
properly for water simulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.800
and for gooey materials is a huge challenge, so&nbsp;
let’s see how traditional methods deal with them!

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:57.360
Experiment number one! Water bunnies. Do you&nbsp;
see what I am seeing here? Did you see the magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:04.240
trick? Let’s look again. Observe how much water we&nbsp;
are starting with. A full bunny worth of water…and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:12.480
then, by the end, we have maybe a quarter of&nbsp;
a bunny left. Oh, yes. We have a substantial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.120
amount of numerical dissipation in the&nbsp;
simulator that leads to volume loss.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:24.080
Can this be solved somehow? Well, let’s see&nbsp;
how this new work deals with this. Starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:32.960
with one bunny…and ending it with one bunny.&nbsp;
Nice! Just look at the difference of the volume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.640
of water left with the new method compared to&nbsp;
the previous one. Night and day difference.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.480
And this was not even the worst volume loss&nbsp;
I’ve seen…make sure to hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:52.880
and check out this one. Experiment number two,&nbsp;
gooey dragons and bowls. When using a traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:02:02.480
technique, whoa, this guy is GONE. And when we try&nbsp;
a different method, it does… this. My goodness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:11.360
So let’s see if the new method can deal&nbsp;
with this case. Oh, yes, yes it can!

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.320
And now, onwards to experiment number&nbsp;
three. If you think that research is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.600
about throwing things at the wall and seeing&nbsp;
what sticks, in the case of this scene…you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:27.120
are not wrong. So what should happen&nbsp;
here, given these materials? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.800
the bunny should stick to the goo,&nbsp;
and not fall too quickly…hm…none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:38.640
of which happens here. The previous method does&nbsp;
not simulate viscosity properly, and hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.480
this artificial melting phenomenon emerges.&nbsp;
I wonder if the new method can do this too?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.520
And, yes! They stick together and the goo&nbsp;
correctly slows down the fall of the bunny.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.280
So how does this magic work? Normally,&nbsp;
in these simulations, we have to compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.520
pressure, viscosity, and frictional contact&nbsp;
separately, which are three different tasks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.480
The technique described in this paper is&nbsp;
called Monolith because it has a monolithic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:17.360
pressure-viscosity-contact solver. Yes, this means&nbsp;
that it does all three of these tasks in one go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:23.440
which is a mathematically a tiny bit more&nbsp;
involved, but it gives us a proper simulator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:30.960
where water and goo can interact with solids.&nbsp;
No volume loss, no artificial melting, no crazy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:38.560
jumpy behavior. And here comes the punchline…I was&nbsp;
thinking that alright, a more accurate simulator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.640
that is always welcome, but what is the price&nbsp;
of this accuracy? How much longer do I have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:51.120
wait? If you have been holding on to your papers,&nbsp;
now squeeze that paper, because this technique is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:57.440
not slower, but up to 10 times faster than&nbsp;
previous methods, and that’s where I fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:03.280
off the chair when reading this paper. And with&nbsp;
this, I hope that we will be able to marvel at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.880
even more delightful two way-coupled simulations&nbsp;
in the near future! What a time to be alive!

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

